{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And Avimelech went to him from Gerar, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say <b>from Gerar</b>, since it is a known thing, and there is no need for this information. And our Rabbis of blessed memory sensed this and expounded (Genesis Rabbah, chapter 64): מְגוֹרָר, etc., and this is the way of derash. Further, why was it necessary to take with him Pikol and the host of his friend, and if it was for the formalities of kingship there would have been no need to write to inform us of things in which we have no need." "<b>Indeed</b> the intent of the verse is to resolve what is difficult for us: why was Avimelech required to pursue Yitzhak to make a covenant with him for no reason, since he had already sworn to him — Avraham, that is — (Genesis 21:23) ״אִם תִּשְׁקֹר לִי וּלְנִינִי וּלְנֶכְדִּי״, and what fear could Avimelech have had concerning Avraham's oath that required an oath from Yitzhak? And the Ramban of blessed memory sensed the need to settle this and said that Avimelech feared that Yitzhak would violate Avraham's guarantee, inasmuch as they had violated the covenant and driven him away from them — thus far. But I have not seen that Avimelech stipulated to Avraham in his oath to dwell in his land, he and his sons, and there is no violation of the covenant in what they did by sending Yitzhak away, and the question still stands in its place: there was no need for a covenant with Yitzhak." "Therefore the verse comes and says <b>from Gerar</b> — meaning: on account of Gerar there is reason to fear the nullification of Avraham's covenant, and they needed to establish a covenant with Yitzhak. And this is along the lines of what they said in Nedarim (65a), and this is their language: It was taught: one who has taken a vow of abstinence from benefit from his fellow is not permitted release except in his presence. And the Hagahot Maimuniyot wrote in chapter 6 of the Laws of Oaths, and this is their language: And our Rabbi Yitzhak said that this is required specifically lekhatekhilah in this matter, but not bedi'avad; and lekhatekhilah they said it only regarding a person who did a kindness for him, such as Moshe, to whom Yitro gave his daughter, and Tzidkiyahu, whom Nevukhadnetzar took out of prison, etc.; but any other person, even lekhatekhilah, as is inferred from the chapter Elu Ne'emarin, etc. (Sotah 36a) — thus far. It is thereby evident, according to this, that the one who swears can rely on his fellow's intention to effect release even lekhatekhilah, even not in his presence — and needless to say bedi'avad, all the more so when he did not receive any benefit from him. And you should know that Avraham received no benefit from Avimelech, for what he gave him as gifts was on account of Sarah's humiliation, and what he gave him to dwell in Gerar — Avraham did not accept; as is evident from the verses, for he turned immediately and went away from Gerar and settled in Be'er Sheva, the place where Yitzhak dwelt after being sent away from Gerar. And moreover there came to my hand one teaching (Genesis Rabbah 54:2), and this is its language: ״כַּחֶסֶד אֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי״, etc. — what kindness did he do with him, that he said to him ״הִנֵּה אַרְצִי לְפָנֶיךָ״, etc., and he did not accept it — thus far. See how Rabbenu conclude that the kindness he did with him — that he said to him ״הִנֵּה אַרְצִי לְפָנֶיךָ״, etc. — he did not accept; and even though he says ״כַּחֶסֶד״, those are his own words and it is not so. But it was not so with Yitzhak, for he settled him in his land, and he dwelt there and prospered in all good. And that is what it says: <b>And Avimelech went to him</b>, and the reason is ״from Gerar״ — meaning: from the legal status drawn from the intermediacy of Gerar. For Avraham had no good benefit from Gerar, since he did not accept it, and his oath is subject to release; whereas Yitzhak had received a benefit, and therefore the oath would stand once it was made for the benefit, and he cannot release it without Avimelech's consent." "<b>Further</b>, even if you were to say that Avimelech, when he made Avraham swear, had an intention contrary to the opinion of our Rabbi Yitzhak that the Hagahot Maimuniyot wrote, and he held like the Rambam who wrote in chapter 6 of the Laws of Oaths that anyone who makes his fellow swear on his own intention, even though he received no benefit from him, is not permitted release except in his presence — even so, he did not rely with full confidence on that oath. For he concedes that if bedi'avad he released his oath, it is released, since no benefit stood in between; and this law is agreed upon by all the decisors. Therefore he went to Yitzhak, who had the benefit of enjoyment, and in this case the Ra'avad and the Rashba and the Aharonim wrote that even if he released bedi'avad it is not released. He also acted cunningly by taking Ahuzat, etc., and Pikol to strengthen the oath, as I shall explain." "<b>And the host of his friend and Pikol, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to take with him the host of his friend — something he did not do in the days of Avraham?" "<b>However,</b> the matter may be explained according to what Rabbeinu Tam was uncertain about (Ran, Nedarim 65a) concerning one who swears upon the understanding of his fellow for the sake of a benefit — if after the fact they released him, whether he is permitted or not, and it was left unresolved. And Avimelech was concerned, according to this reasoning, also for this oath, even though it was for the sake of a benefit, and he was cunning to make a reinforcement so that the release (hatarah) would not avail him. And this is along the lines of what they said in Gittin, page 46a, the dispute of Rabbi Yehudah and the Rabbis: Rabbi Yehudah holds that a vow uttered in public has no release, since once he vowed in the presence of the public he can no longer obtain release; and the Rabbis hold he can obtain release. And they said in the Gemara, page 36a, Ameimar said: even according to the one who says a vow uttered in public does have release, a vow uttered upon the understanding of the public has no release — end of quote. And the Tosafot wrote that there is room to say that Ameimar did not set aside the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, who said a vow uttered in the presence of the public has no release. And also from the words of Rav Yosef (Pesachim 107a) it is likewise proven that the halachah follows Rabbi Yehudah, and in the conclusion of their discussion they concluded that the halachah follows the Rabbis, and the Geonim ruled according to Rabbi Yehudah." "And according to this, Avimelech was cunning to bring many people with him — ״אחזת [Ahuzzat his friend] and Phichol״ — so that he would vow in the presence of the public. And behold, the Amoraim disagreed in Gittin (page 46a), and this is their language: ״If the public knew about it, how many constitute the public? Rav Nachman said three, Rabbi Yitzchak said ten.״ And I glanced at the words of our Sages of blessed memory, who disagreed in Bereshit Rabbah — Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Nechemiah — and this is their language: Rabbi Yehudah says, Ahuzzat — his friend's name; Rabbi Nechemiah says, the retinue of those who love him — end of quote. And perhaps their dispute depends on what the Amoraim disagreed about: one said three and one said ten. Rabbi Yehudah, who said ״Ahuzzat is his name,״ holds that the multitude is — three, and therefore he said ״Ahuzzat is his name.״ And Rabbi Nechemiah holds ten, therefore he said ״the retinue of״ etc., for with this they were ten, for the purpose of the intention that it be upon the understanding of the public, which has no release, according to the opinion of Rabbi Yitzchak." "<b>And it is possible</b> that he was cunning also according to the opinion of the Rabbis, who said that release is available — and for this he brought Phichol, according to the words of Ameimar that the Rabbis concede regarding a vow upon the understanding of the public that there is no release; for this he brought Phichol, as Rabbi Nechemiah said: ״Pi (the mouth of) all his hosts״ etc. — end of quote. And Avimelech swore Yitzchak upon the understanding of all his hosts." "<b>And I observed</b> that Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Nechemiah, who disagreed on the interpretation of ״Ahuzzat״ and the rest, also disagreed (Bereshit Rabbah 64:9) on the interpretation of ״Phichol.״ And it is possible that there is one reason for both disputes: Rabbi Yehudah, who holds ״Ahuzzat״ is his name, and holds that since Avimelech's intent was that the covenant be in the presence of the public, and the public is three, therefore Scripture left it according to its plain sense — three are the public: Avimelech, Phichol, and Ahuzzat. And Rabbi Nechemiah, for whom three did not suffice to be called the public, and interpreted ״Ahuzzat״ as the retinue of those who love him — if so, why did he bring this Phichol? And if he did bring him, why did Scripture need to mention him? For the ten are included in the retinue, since a retinue is no fewer than ten. Therefore he interpreted ״Pi (the mouth of) all״ etc., and to another measure he intended — that it be upon the understanding of the public, as I have written. And let it not be difficult in your eyes that we explained the Tannaim disagreeing as the Amoraim disagreed — such is the way of the public — for the Amoraim were not experts in the beraitot, and especially in the aggadot."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {6 "The editor's citation 'ר״ן נדרים סה.' likely refers to the Ran's commentary on Nedarim 65a, where Rabbeinu Tam's query is discussed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:14.212778556Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}